        },
        {
          "text": "examples/generation_loop.py exposes the low-level inference contract: construct Config, Model, and Cache before model.load(); tokenize the prompt; prefill all but its final token; then forward that final token with past_len equal to context length minus one. Each sampled token is appended and becomes the next input. The example supplies flash_attn, cache, and a rectangular batch_shape of (1, 2048), checks token stop conditions, and streams vocabulary pieces. examples/generator.py offers the higher-level alternative: generate() for complete single/batched outputs, or enqueue Job objects and route iterate() result chunks by identifier. Its temperature sweep demonstrates a sampler option, not a quality evaluation.",
          "basis": "source",
          "evidence": ["call_c3116b3fe85645bdb4972833dec8beee", "call_286847ff6d4843ee92854aadfd7f95ac"]
        },
        {
          "text": "The interactive entrypoint is python examples/chat.py -m <model_dir> -mode <mode>. It formats and retokenizes conversation history, reserves room for the response, removes oldest turns when necessary, and streams a Job's text updates. chat_templates.py supplies raw, llama3, chatml, phi, mistral, and gemma formats with explicit BOS and stop-token policies; these are application-side templates, not architecture validation. The separate common.py helper labels its templates approximate, contains an extra literal 'n' in the Mistral prompt, and has no Mistral branch in get_stop_conditions.",
          "basis": "source",
